{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017UZqHkuXQQSzT3aTdQeLgF","type":"tool_result","content":"195:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n196:5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and the route's label (none under `route: none`); otherwise they start a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - global open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n207:**Closed is not always \"forget\".** Removing a fingerprint on every close, as first sketched, refiles a won't-fix issue on the next run. Completed closes stop tracking the finding but keep a `fixed` tombstone, so a finding that comes back is filed as a regression of the old issue. Not-planned closes, and closes as a duplicate, suppress, and re-raise early if severity rises.\n227:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n233:**The missing-tests scanner** (`scanners/missing-tests`, a `sh` script plus a stdlib-only Go helper) runs each Go module's own tests with `go test -coverpkg=./...` and files one finding per exported function or method that no test calls by name or that the tests never run; the group is the package, and the fingerprint does not depend on the file or line. Because it runs the repository's tests it needs the executor's container and egress to the module proxy. If a module's tests fail to build or fail, the scan fails and files nothing, so autoclose never acts on partial coverage. A human-owned `ignore` file next to `scan` skips symbols or packages, and a `no-coverage` file there skips the test run and reports only the static rule. It ships in the starter tree but is not enabled on ard's own `.ard/`.\n241:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n284:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n288:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n305:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n340:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n393:  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n420:Configuration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n481:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can re