{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNKhbkdwuvyR9vmgQhrZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MjjgFfpxVu1q63ZHoM7P6N","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/proposal.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep('''    message: The loop skips the last element.   # required, non-empty\n---''','''    message: The loop skips the last element.   # required, non-empty\nfetched:              # workers only, optional: at most 50 (#300)\n  - url: https://endoflife.date/api/alpine-linux.json   # https only, required\n    date: 2026-11-23            # YYYY-MM-DD, required\n    status: 200                 # HTTP status, required\n    sha256: 1295…               # 64 lowercase hex, required\n    verified_by: https://…/checksums.txt   # https URL, or none (unverified); required\n    path: internal/roles/testdata/lts/api/alpine-linux.json   # repo-relative landing path, required\n---''')\nrep('''A worker's inputs are `.ard-input''','''**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n\nA worker's inputs are `.ard-input''')\nrep('''agent jobs get only their model credential.''','''an agent job's environment holds its model credential and nothing else of value, and with `network: true` (the default) it may reach the network; see the `network` setting under Configuration for what is and is not enforced.''')\nrep('''the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard''','''the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard''')\nrep('''in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.''','''egress is not enforced by ard in any executor (see `network` under Configuration), and no network policy in the chart limits it in this part (the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296, a human change).''')\nrep('''and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.''','''and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n\nWhat `true` and `false` mean per executor, as the code does today: the host executor only sets `ARD_NETWORK` and the prompt line, and logs that `false` is not enforced; the Docker executor passes the same variable and prompt line and adds no network option to `docker run`, so the container has the default bridge network either way. Nothing blocks egress for either value, so `false` is a request the agent may ignore, not a boundary. Risks of a role with network (#276): (1) exfiltration of anything the agent can read, including its model credential and the checked-out source; (2) fetched content that carries prompt injection; (3) fetched bytes that change between runs, so results do not reproduce; (4) a compromised or typosquatted source; (5) fetching code or data that lands in the repository unreviewed; (6) a download that is large or slow and burns the job's time and spend; (7) a request to an internal or metadata address from the host or cluster. The residual risk is that none of these is closed by the setting: the diff guard, the reviewers, the `fetched:` records (URL, hash and verifier for every landed input) and the missing GitHub token limit the damage, and a human reviews every PR. Fetched bytes are untrusted data, never instructions, and are not a reason to trust the source. For roles with network, use a dedicated workspace API key with a spend cap, so a stolen or abused key can cost only a bounded amount.''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -c \"only their model credential\\|uses no other network\" docs/proposal.md; git -c core.excludesFile=/dev/null status --porcelain --untracked-files=all"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":392,"cache_read_input_tokens":48996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":392},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c582290-3920-45a2-873b-e6fcce9b450f","uuid":"8b9ce0a9-c163-482b-94d7-c6c4fec63a28","timestamp":"2026-11-24T19:42:15.593Z","request_id":"req_011ChNKhbcDT4o6yEmPJyyqo"}
